From 1f1df8fbf2889c9d0d02dc2204a965ddcde4a40a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Noah Evans <noah.evans@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 07:08:53 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] hw/arm/stm32f405_soc: flash comes up erased, not zeroed

Flash comes out of the factory erased, which is all ones, and a rom
region is zero filled.  Anything a guest read outside what the loader
wrote therefore read 0x00 here and 0xff on silicon.

That is not a detail.  Erased flash is how a guest tells written from
unwritten without a header or a superblock.
os/inferno/stm32f407/calstm32f4.c keeps an append-only log in the tail of
the last sector -- the part has no spare sector, the image ends inside
sector 10 and sector 11 is the bootloader -- and finds its end by looking
for the first 0xff, because the content is attrdb(6) text which never
contains one.  Against this model that store read

	base 0x080dc000 size 16384 used 16384 free 0

full, on a board that had never written a byte of it.  On silicon the
same code reads used 0.

Filled before the kernel is loaded, so the loader still wins wherever the
image covers.

What this does NOT model is programming: the region stays rom, so a guest
write is dropped.  The driver catches that by reading back what it wrote
-- "cal: wrote 0x72 at offset 0 and it reads 0xff" -- which is the right
answer for a write that did not happen, so the read path and the refusals
are exercisable here and the write path is silicon only.

Signed-off-by: Noah Evans <noah.evans@gmail.com>
---
 hw/arm/stm32f405_soc.c | 18 ++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 18 insertions(+)

diff --git a/hw/arm/stm32f405_soc.c b/hw/arm/stm32f405_soc.c
index 3826daa..8773d93 100644
--- a/hw/arm/stm32f405_soc.c
+++ b/hw/arm/stm32f405_soc.c
@@ -169,6 +169,24 @@ static void stm32f405_soc_realize(DeviceState *dev_soc, Error **errp)
                              "STM32F405.flash.alias", &s->flash, 0,
                              FLASH_SIZE);
 
+    /*
+     * Flash comes out of the factory ERASED, which is all ones, and a
+     * rom region is zero filled.  Anything a guest reads outside what
+     * the loader wrote therefore read 0x00 here and 0xff on silicon.
+     *
+     * That is not a detail.  Erased flash is how a guest tells written
+     * from unwritten without a header or a superblock -- os/inferno/
+     * stm32f407/calstm32f4.c keeps an append-only log in the tail of
+     * the last sector and finds its end by looking for the first 0xff,
+     * because the content is attrdb(6) text which never contains one.
+     * Against this model that store read "used 16384 free 0": full, on
+     * a board that had never written a byte of it.
+     *
+     * Filled before the kernel is loaded, so the loader still wins
+     * wherever the image covers.
+     */
+    memset(memory_region_get_ram_ptr(&s->flash), 0xff, FLASH_SIZE);
+
     memory_region_add_subregion(system_memory, FLASH_BASE_ADDRESS, &s->flash);
     memory_region_add_subregion(system_memory, 0, &s->flash_alias);
 
-- 
